#{[17592186216957 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+2x+5$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 2^2-4(1)(5) = -16 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=2$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)({0}^{2}+2(0)+5) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c7ef60b2b4e65b4f62c30bedd785608bbc3dd2daad8463c8d979897549c413e.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n0 \\leq a \\leq 2\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186216992 :question/worked-solution "We are told that\n\n$$\nd(x) = \\dfrac{r(x)}{x + 1}\n$$\n\nand that $r$ is a linear function. To isolate $r(x)$, we multiply both sides of the equation by $(x + 1){:}$\n\n$$\nr(x) = d(x) \\cdot (x + 1)\n$$\n\nUsing the values from the table, we compute $r(x)$ at three $x$-values.\n\n- When $x = -3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-3) & =d(-3)\\cdot (-3+1) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- When $x = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =d(1)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- When $x = 3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =d(3)\\cdot (3+1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have three points on the graph of $y=r(x)$, namely, $(-3, 12)$, $(1, 4)$, and $(3, 0)$.\n\nSince $r$ is linear, we want to find the equation representing the line that passes through these points in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nWe use the two given points, say\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (-3, 12)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (1, 4)\n$$\n\nto find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (4 - 12)/(1 - (-3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{4} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute any point the line passes through, such as $(3, 0)$, into the equation to solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2x + b \\\\\n0 &= -2(3) + b \\\\\nb &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line $y=r(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny = -2x + 6\n$$\n\nThe $y$-intercept is the value of $r(0)$, which we evaluate by substituting $x=0$ into the obtained equation:\n\n$$\nr(0) = -2(0) + 6 = 6\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = r(x)$ is $(0, 6)$."] [17592186216993 :question/worked-solution "When a fixed constant is added to every data value, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $29$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $15$th value) of $3.4$. Adding $0.3$ to every value shifts the median to $3.7$. So, the median increases by $0.3$.\n- The mean depends on every value, so adding the same number to all values will increase the mean by the same number. If we denote the $29$ different weights by $w_1, w_2, \\ldots, w_{29}$ and add $0.3$ to each, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({w}_{1}+0.3)+({w}_{2}+0.3)+⋯+({w}_{29}+0.3)}{29} & =\\frac{0.3\\cdot 29+({w}_{1}+{w}_{2}+⋯+{w}_{29})}{29} \\\\  & =0.3+\\frac{{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}+⋯+{w}_{29}}{29} \\\\  & =0.3+3.4 \\\\  & =3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the mean also increases by $0.3$.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Since both of these values are shifted by the same amount, the range remains unchanged.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since adding the same constant to every value (and thus to the mean) does not change the deviations from the mean, the standard deviation is unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [17592186216996 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our rectangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The length is $l = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the width by $w$. We are told that the length is $7$ centimeters less than three times its width:\n$x = 3w - 7$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3w-7 \\\\ x+7 & =3w \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{3} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= wl \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x(x + 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216997 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = 7$ feet.\n- The length is $l = x$ feet.\n- Let the width be $w$. We are told that the length is $5$ feet more than $3$ times the width:\n$x = 3w + 5$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3w+5 \\\\ x-5 & =3w \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{3} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= lwh \\\\\n&= x \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}x(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216998 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The radius of the base is $r = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the height by $h$. We're told that the base radius is $4$ centimeters less than one-third the height. So, we have\n$x = \\frac{1}{3}h-4$.\nSolving for $h$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}h-4 \\\\ x+4 & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ 3(x+4) & =h \\\\ h & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot 3(x + 4) \\\\\n&= πx^{2}(x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186217606 :question/problem "A cylinder has a height of $h$ and a base radius of $r$. The second cylinder has an identical base but is two times higher. The two cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Which of the following gives the surface area of the combined solid?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186217608 :question/problem "Two identical cylinders have a height of $10$ centimeters and a base radius of $5$ centimeters. The cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Find the surface area, in square centimeters, of the combined solid.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*\n\nThe surface area of the combined solid is\n{{field-1}}\n$cm^{2}$."] [17592186217610 :question/problem "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $15$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186218242 :tutorial/content "Finally, we'll discuss how to bound the *difference* between two statistics. This is often useful when comparing two data sets, and we want to see how large or small differences in their metrics could be.\n\n![](images/89/898b8ceb1d145a843b51ff173c5471675b403189781793fc6156222c844d4c23.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. What is the *largest possible difference* between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$? We can write this symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B) - \\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the *largest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *largest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *smallest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the largest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our data set as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the *largest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.\nValue $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{max}}$$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$$80-84$$2$$84$$2\\cdot 84 = 168$$84-88$$3$$88$$3\\cdot 88 = 264$$88-92$$7$$92$$7\\cdot 92 = 644$$92-96$$5$$96$$5\\cdot 96 = 480$$96-100$$3$$100$$3\\cdot 100 = 300$\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}max(\\text{mean}(B)) & =\\frac{168+264+644+480+300}{2+3+7+5+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1856}{20} \\\\  & =92.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Next, we compute the smallest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $36 - 39$. Therefore, the *smallest* possible value of the median is\n$\\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 36$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the largest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(mean(B)) - min(median(A)) &= 92.8 - 36 \\\\\n&= 56.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186218245 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write exponential functions in many different, equivalent forms. Depending on the situation, we may prefer one form over another if it displays characteristics we're interested in more clearly.\n\nFor example, consider the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=35\\cdot(1.4)^{x+1}\n$$\n\nIt's possible to express this function in a variety of different forms using the laws of exponents. Let's go through some of the most common:\n\n- We can write the function $f$ in an equivalent form with $x$ only in the exponent using the product rule in reverse.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =35\\cdot (1.4)^{x+1} \\\\  & =35\\cdot (1.4)^{x}\\cdot (1.4)^{1} \\\\  & =35\\cdot (1.4)^{x}\\cdot 1.4 \\\\  & =35\\cdot 1.4\\cdot (1.4)^{x} \\\\  & =49\\cdot (1.4)^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Similarly, we can write the function $f$ in an equivalent form with $(x-1)$ in the exponent as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =49\\cdot (1.4)^{x} \\\\  & =49\\cdot (1.4)^{x-1+1} \\\\  & =49\\cdot (1.4)^{x-1}\\cdot (1.4)^{1} \\\\  & =49\\cdot (1.4)^{x-1}\\cdot 1.4 \\\\  & =49\\cdot 1.4\\cdot (1.4)^{x-1} \\\\  & =68.6\\cdot (1.4)^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f$ has the following equivalent forms:\n\n- $f(x) = 35\\cdot(1.4)^{x+1}$\n- $f(x) = 49 \\cdot (1.4)^x$\n- $f(x) = 68.6 \\cdot (1.4)^{x-1}$\n\nPlease note that exponential functions have *many* equivalent forms. For the function $f$ here, this is only three of them."] [17592186218260 :tutorial/content "We now consider a slightly more involved function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2 \\cdot m^x + n\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Suppose we are told that the graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0, 1\\right)\n$$\n\nand the product of $m$ and $n$ is $-12$.\n\nLet's use this information to find the values of $m$ and $n$. To do this, we examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, 1\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = 1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $n{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ 2\\cdot {m}^{0}+n & =1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+n & =1 \\\\ 2+n & =1 \\\\ 2+n-2 & =1-2 \\\\ n & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Since $mn=-12$ and using the fact that $n = -1$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $m{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}mn & =-12 \\\\ m(-1) & =-12 \\\\ -m & =-12 \\\\ (-m)(-1) & =(-12)(-1) \\\\ m & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $m=12$ and $n=-1$."] [17592186218318 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll learn how to determine which values of a polynomial $p(x)$ are possible over a specified $x$-interval without needing to compute exact outputs or plot the full graph.\n\nFor example, in the $xy$-plane, the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny=2(x+1)(x-1)(x-3)\n$$\n\ncontains the point $(a,b)$, where\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWhat can we say about the possible values of $b$ on this interval?\n\nFirst, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-1,1,3$.\n- Since the leading coefficient is positive, we have:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y → ∞ & as & x → ∞ \\\\ y → - ∞ & as & x → - ∞\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0+1)(0-1)(0-3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c56df4301167d81ecc6eb3caaf58f3e5048033eb0b6eaf14110249ccd8d69d8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThis interval is highlighted on the graph.\n\nNotice that on this interval, the graph of the cubic lies on or above the $x$-axis (it meets the axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ and is positive in between). So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b$ can't be negative."] [17592186218326 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our original dataset:\n$4, \\quad 6, \\quad 7, \\quad 8, \\quad 9$\n\nRecall that:\n\n- The mean is $6.8$.\n- The median is $7$.\n- The range is $5$.\n\nNow, suppose that we \"push away from the median\" by adding $2$ to every value above the median and subtracting $2$ from every value below the median, so the new dataset is\n${\\color{blue}2}, \\; {\\color{blue}4}, \\; 7, \\; {\\color{blue}10}, \\; {\\color{blue}11}$.\n\nLet's discuss how this transformation affects the statistics we found earlier:\n\n- The *mean* remains the same! This is because the change in the sum is balanced on either side of the median. So, if we compute the net change between the old dataset and the new one, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Change in sum} & =({\\color{blue}{2}}-4)+({\\color{blue}{4}}-6)+(7-7)+({\\color{blue}{10}}-8)+({\\color{blue}{11}}-9) \\\\  & =(-2)+(-2)+0+2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nComputing the new mean explicitly, we have\n$\\dfrac{2+4+7+10+11}{5} = 6.8$.\n- The *median* also remains the same. Each value is pushed away from the median ($7$), so their order relative to it does change, and neither does the middle value.\n- The *range* increases because the new data set is formed by pushing away from the median, increasing the dataset's spread. Note that the new range is\n$11 - 2 = 9$.\n- The *standard deviation* increases since the data are more spread out.\n\nLet's see an example where we multiply each member of a data set by a fixed number."] [17592186218342 :tutorial/content "Consider the following functions, each defined on\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\n- $f(x) = 2\\cdot(0.5)^x$\n- $g(x) = 4\\cdot(2.5)^x$\n- $h(x) = 4\\cdot(2.5)^x+1$\n\nOn the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a> 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its minimum at $x=0$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=2\\cdot(0.5)^x$,\nthe base is $0.5\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 0$ and has *no minimum*. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function $g(x)=4\\cdot(2.5)^x$, the base is $2.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =4\\cdot (2.5)^{0} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the minimum value is $4$, which is exactly the coefficient shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=4\\cdot(2.5)^x+1$,\nthe base is $2.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(0) & =4\\cdot (2.5)^{0}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe minimum value is $5$, which is *not* equal to the coefficient ($4$) or the constant term ($1$). Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the minimum as a constant term or a coefficient."] [17592186220968 :question/worked-solution "We compare the volumes of two cylinders using their formulas, given that the second pipe’s volume is $162$ times greater than the first. Recall that the volume of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nLet the volume of the smaller pipe be $V_1{:}$\n\n$$\nV_1 = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe are told the second pipe has $162$ times the volume of the first pipe, so its volume, $V_2$, is\n\n$$\nV_2 = 162 \\cdot \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSuppose the second pipe has radius $R$ and height $H$. Its volume is\n\n$$\nV_2 = \\pi R^2 H\n$$\n\nNow, we set the two expressions for $V_2$ equal and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπR^{2}H &= 162 \\cdot πr^{2}h \\\\\nR^{2}H &= 162r^{2}h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we test the choices to find which pair of values for $R$ and $H$ makes this equation true.\n\n- For $R = 36r$ and $H = \\dfrac{1}{2}h{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}H & =(36r)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h \\\\  & =1296{r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h \\\\  & =648{r}^{2}h\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 4h{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}H & =(6r)^{2}\\cdot 4h \\\\  & =36{r}^{2}\\cdot 4h \\\\  & =144{r}^{2}h\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 12r$ and $H = \\dfrac{9}{5}h{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}H & =(12r)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h \\\\  & =144{r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h \\\\  & =259.2{r}^{2}h\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 18h{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}H & =(6r)^{2}\\cdot 18h \\\\  & =36{r}^{2}\\cdot 18h \\\\  & =648{r}^{2}h\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n\n- For $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}H & =(9r)^{2}\\cdot 2h \\\\  & =81{r}^{2}\\cdot 2h \\\\  & =162{r}^{2}h\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis matches the given equation.\n\nSo, the correct answer is $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h$."] [17592186220975 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nWe are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y - (-4))^2 = 4^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the center of our circle has coordinates $(7,-4)$, and its radius is $4$.\n\nNow, for a point to be inside a circle, the distance from that point to the center of the circle must be less than the radius.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to find which of the given points is such that its distance to $(7,-4)$ is less than $4$ (the radius of the circle).\n\n- For $(7,-4)$, this is the center of the circle, and therefore must lie in its interior.\n- For $(12,-4)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(12-7)^{2}+(-4-(-4))^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & ≮4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $(9,-4)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(9-7)^{2}+(-4-(-4))^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186221014 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $y$ be the volume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25.0\\mathrm{\\%}\\times 90.0\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{mL} & =0.25\\times 90.0\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{mL} \\\\  & =22.50\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{mL}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe are also told that the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt. Therefore, we get the equation\n$0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50$.\n\nPutting the equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 90.0 \\\\ 0.10 x + 0.40 y = 22.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 90.0 \\\\\ny &= 90.0 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\ny = 90.0 - x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x + 0.40y &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 0.40(90.0 - x) &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 36.0 - 0.40x &= 22.50 \\\\\n- 0.30x &= -13.50 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-13.50}{-0.30} \\\\\nx &= 45.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt by volume, the volume of salt in the first solution used was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x &= 0.10(45.0) \\\\\n&= 4.5mL\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221018 :question/worked-solution "Recall that each equation of a linear system defines a straight line. The solution to the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nLet’s find the equations of the lines.\n\n- For the first (orange) line, we pick two points on the line.\nFor example, this line passes through $(0, -5)$ and $(1, -3)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\frac{-3 - (-5)}{1 - 0} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2\n$$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, -5)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5 & =2(x-0) \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-5 \\\\ -2x+y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the second (blue) line, we pick two points on the line.\nThis line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, -1)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\frac{-1 - 0}{2 - 0} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, 0)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x\\end{aligned}$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\ 2y & =-x \\\\ x+2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system that matches the graph is the following:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=-5 \\\\ x + 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**"] [17592186221020 :question/worked-solution "To determine which graph represents this system, we first rewrite each equation in slope-intercept form.\n\n- For the first equation, we have the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =-3 \\\\ 3y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis line has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and a $y$-intercept of $-1$.\n- For the second equation, we have the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5y & =10 \\\\ -5y & =-2x+10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis line has a slope of $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ and a $y$-intercept of $-2$.\n\nNow, we draw both lines on the same graph.\n\n- For the first line, $y = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x - 1{:}$If $x = 0$, then $y = -1$.If $y = 0$, then $x = -3$.So, the line passes through points $(0, -1)$ and $(-3, 0)$.\n- For the second line, $y = \\dfrac{2}{5}x - 2{:}$If $x = 0$, then $y = -2$.If $y = 0$, then $x = 5$.So, the line passes through points $(0, -2)$ and $(5, 0)$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following graph, where the intersection of the lines represents the solution to our system.\n\n![](images/2c/2ca4fd9da55dfbe61ead9f6b933d2a85a5a72cacbd59fadb9b6c1fafbb84bbdd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is graph II."] [17592186221026 :question/worked-solution "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle A + m\\angle B + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles, it must have at least two equal angles. Let's consider the distinct possibilities.\n\n- If $m\\angle B = m\\angle A = 70^\\circ$, then $70^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n- If $m\\angle B = m\\angle C$, then we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}A+m\\mathrm{∠}B+m\\mathrm{∠}C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}A+m\\mathrm{∠}B+m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}A+2m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {70}^{∘}+2m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, $55^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n- If $m\\angle C = m\\angle A = 70^\\circ$, then we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}A+m\\mathrm{∠}B+m\\mathrm{∠}C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {70}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}B+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {140}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, $40^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n\nTherefore, among the choices, only $62^\\circ$ is not a possible measure of $\\angle B$."] [17592186221036 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-4x+1 \\\\ mx+2ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{m}{2n}x+\\frac{1}{2n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{m}{2n} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{m}{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-4x+1 \\\\ mx-ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=\\frac{m}{n}x-\\frac{1}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-2y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=4x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx+ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{2m}{n}x+\\frac{1}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2m}{n} & =-2\\cdot \\frac{m}{n} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186221064 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- First equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x+10y & =-25 \\\\ 10y & =-15x-25 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-15}{10}x+\\frac{-25}{10} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Second equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-8qy & =\\frac{7}{5}+6x \\\\ (1-8q)y & =6x+\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{(1-8q)}x+\\frac{7}{5(1-8q)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this equation to have the same slope as the first equation, we match the slopes and solve for $q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6)/((1 - 8q)) &= -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= -3(1 - 8q) \\\\\n12 &= -3 + 24q \\\\\n24q &= 15 \\\\\nq &= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the system has no solution for\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$"] [17592186221065 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\nm = kw + b\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept:\n\n- First equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-9m & =aw \\\\ -9m & =aw-7 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{a}{9}w+\\frac{7}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Second equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9m+6w & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -9m & =-6w-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ m & =\\frac{2}{3}w+\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{9}\\right.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, we match the slopes and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{a}{9} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n- a &= 6 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221099 :question/worked-solution "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3y & =9 \\\\ -3y & =-6x+9 \\\\ 3y & =6x-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{6x-9}{3} \\\\ y & =2x-3 \\\\ y & =2t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $(t,\\, 2t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $x$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3y & =9 \\\\ 6x & =3y+9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3y+9}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t + \\dfrac{3}{2},\\, t\\right),\n$$ which is *not* the same as $(3t-9,\\, t)$. Hence, $(3t-9,\\, t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(4t,h(t))$. So, we set $x=4t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y=9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3y & =9 \\\\ -3y & =-6x+9 \\\\ 3y & =6x-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{6x-9}{3} \\\\ y & =2x-3 \\\\ y & =2(4t)-3 \\\\ y & =8t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich holds for all $t$. Hence, $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I and III.**"] [17592186221129 :question/problem "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $12$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186224846 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9m+14 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2}\\text{ }\\underbrace{+\\text{ }7m+2m}_{9m}+14 & =0 \\\\ m(m+7)+2(m+7) & =0 \\\\ (m+7)(m+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & m+7=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-7 \\\\  &  & m+2=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle r =-7ands =-2\n$$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+5m+4 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2}\\text{ }\\underbrace{+\\text{ }m+4m}_{5m}+4 & =0 \\\\ m(m+1)+4(m+1) & =0 \\\\ (m+1)(m+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & m+1=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-1 \\\\  &  & m+4=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle t =-4andu =-1\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224880 :question/worked-solution "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} + 4 ) \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} + 4 \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {k}^{2} + 4 \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}-{k}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={k}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}-{k}^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{k}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={k}^{2}+16 \\\\ r & =\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16-{k}^{2}}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16-{k}^{2}}}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{\\sqrt{16}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution."] [17592186224881 :question/worked-solution "To solve for $x$, we first combine like terms and square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 - \\sqrt{x - 2} &= \\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 2\\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3x} + 1)^{2} &= (2\\sqrt{x - 2})^{2} \\\\\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting equation still contains a radical term. To solve, we isolate this term and square both sides again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4x - 8 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{3x} &= x - 9 \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{3x})^{2} &= (x - 9)^{2} \\\\\n12x &= x^{2} - 18x + 81 \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} - 30x + 81 \\\\\nx^{2} - 30x + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 27)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=27$ and $x=3$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=27$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 27}+1-\\sqrt{27-2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{27-2} \\\\ \\sqrt{81}+1-\\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{25} \\\\ 9+1-5 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $x=27$ is a valid solution.\n- For $x=3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3}+1-\\sqrt{3-2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3-2} \\\\ \\sqrt{9}+1-\\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{1} \\\\ 3+1-1 & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 3 & \\ne 1.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $x=3$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x}+1-\\sqrt{x-2}=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nis $x=27$ only."] [17592186224882 :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the given equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{4q + 28})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{(q - 1)^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n4q + 28 &= (q - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, expanding the square, moving all terms to one side, and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4q + 28 &= q^{2} - 2q + 1 \\\\\n0 &= q^{2} - 6q - 27 \\\\\n0 &= (q + 3)(q - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two possible solutions: $q=-3$ or $q=9$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions, by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $q=-3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4(-3)+28} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{((-3)-1)^{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{-12+28} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{16}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $q=-3$ *is* a valid solution.\n- For $q=9$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4(9)+28} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{(9-1)^{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{36+28} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{{8}^{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{64} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{64}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $q=9$ *is* a valid solution.\n\nSo, the valid solutions are $q=-3$ and $q=9$.\nTherefore, the smallest real solution is $q=-3$."] [17592186224900 :question/worked-solution "We are given a system of equations involving a quadratic and a linear equation. To solve, we substitute the second equation into the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= -11u + v \\\\\nu^{2} &= -11u + (11u + 121) \\\\\nu^{2} &= 121\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides to solve for $u{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{u^{2}} &= \\sqrt{121} \\\\\n\\mid u \\mid &= 11 \\\\\nu &= ± 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute each $u$-value into the second equation to find the corresponding $v$-values:\n\n- If $u = 11$, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =11u+121 \\\\  & =11(11)+121 \\\\  & =242.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $u = -11$, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =11(-11)+121 \\\\  & =-121+121 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $uv$ in each case:\n\n- If $u = 11,\\ v = 242$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle uv = 11 \\cdot 242 = 2662.\n$$\n- If $u = -11,\\ v = 0$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle uv = -11 \\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$\n\nAmong the given answer choices, the only possible value of $uv$ is $0$."] [17592186224947 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $q$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $q$ must have the form\n\n$$\nq(x) = a \\cdot k^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $k$ is the growth factor.\n\nWe will use the values from the table to write a system of equations for the parameters $a$ and $k$.\n\n- From $x = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & ={b}^{-7} \\\\ a\\cdot {k}^{0} & ={b}^{-7} \\\\ a & ={b}^{-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- From $x = 2$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2) & ={b}^{3} \\\\ a\\cdot {k}^{2} & ={b}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= b^{-7} \\\\\na \\cdot k^{2} &= b^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a \\cdot k^{2}}{a} &= \\frac{b^{3}}{b^{-7}} \\\\\nk^{2} &= b^{10} \\\\\nk &= b^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a=b^{-7}$ and $k=b^{5}$ into the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(x) &= a \\cdot k^{x} \\\\\n&= b^{-7} \\cdot (b^{5})^{x} \\\\\n&= b^{-7} \\cdot b^{5x} \\\\\n&= b^{5x - 7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that\n\n$$\nq(x) = b^{5x - 7}\n$$\n\nworks for $x = 3{:}$\n\n$$\nq(3) = b^{5(3)-7}=b^{8}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the value in the table.\n\nWe are asked to find $m$ such that\n\n$$\nq(m) = b^{23}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nq(m) = b^{5m - 7}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nb^{5m - 7} = b^{23}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate the exponents and solve for $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5m - 7 &= 23 \\\\\n5m &= 30 \\\\\nm &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224948 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $g$ is a quadratic function, so the relationship between $x$ and $g$ must have the form\n\n$$\ng(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nWe will use the values from the table to write a system of equations for the parameters $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n\n- From $x = -2$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =0 \\\\ a(-2)^{2}+b(-2)+c & =0 \\\\ 4a-2b+c & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- From $x = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & =-3 \\\\ a(1)^{2}+b(1)+c & =-3 \\\\ a+b+c & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- From $x = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =2 \\\\ a(0)^{2}+b(0)+c & =2 \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $c = 2$ into the other two equations.\n\n- From $4a - 2b + c = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a-2b+2 & =0 \\\\ 4a-2b & =-2 \\\\ 2a-b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- From $a + b + c = -3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b+2 & =-3 \\\\ a+b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - b &= -1 \\\\\na + b &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= -6 \\\\\na &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the value $a=-2$ into\n\n$$\na + b = -5{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n-2 + b = -5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b = -3\n$$\n\nFinally, collecting $a=-2$, $b=-3$, and $c=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= ax^{2} + bx + c \\\\\n&=-2x^{2} - 3x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224995 :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-17y-15 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\underbrace{-\\text{ }20y+3y}_{-17y}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4y(y-5)+3(y-5) & =0 \\\\ (y-5)(4y+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & y-5=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=5 \\\\  &  & 4y+3=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."] [17592186225011 :question/worked-solution "To solve for $x$, we first square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x - x^{2}})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{-3x - 5})^{2} \\\\\nx - x^{2} &= -3x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move all terms to one side and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 5 &= x - x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=-1$ and $x=5$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=-1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-1-(-1)^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{-3(-1)-5} \\\\ \\sqrt{-1-1} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3-5} \\\\ \\sqrt{-2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{-2},\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-{5}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{-3(5)-5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5-25} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{-15-5} \\\\ \\sqrt{-20} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{-20},\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=5$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-x^2}=\\sqrt{-3x-5}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions."] [17592186225016 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=13 \\\\ y &=\\mid - 3x + 4 \\mid\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $y = 13$.\n\nSubstituting $y = 13$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\mid -3x + 4 \\mid = 13\n$$\n\nThis absolute value equation has two cases:\n\n- If $-3x + 4 > 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =13 \\\\ -3x & =9 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $-3x + 4 < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =-13 \\\\ -3x & =-17 \\\\ x & =\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = -3$ and\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{17}{3}\n$$\n\nare the two solutions of the given system of equations."] [17592186225024 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, with its maximum at $x=0$, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, and has no maximum.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=18\\cdot(1.2)^x$,\nthe base is $1.2>1$. So, the function has no maximum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n- For the function $g(x)=27\\cdot(0.75)^x$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =27\\cdot (0.75)^{0} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the maximum value is $27$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the maximum as a coefficient.\n- For the function $h(x)=9\\cdot(0.75)^x+4$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(0) & =9\\cdot (0.75)^{0}+4 \\\\  & =9+4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the maximum value is $13$. However, neither the constant term ($4$) nor the coefficient ($9$) equals $13$. Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186225027 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its **minimum** at $x=1$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=8\\cdot(0.7)^x+2$,\nthe base is $0.7\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 1$ and has **no** minimum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function\n$g(x)=12\\cdot(1.4)^x-3$,\nthe base is $1.4>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & =12\\cdot (1.4)^{1}-3 \\\\  & =12\\cdot 1.4-3 \\\\  & =16.8-3 \\\\  & =13.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe minimum value is $13.8$, which is **not** equal to the coefficient ($12$) or the constant term $(-3)$. Hence, the equation of $g$ does *not* display the minimum.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=15\\cdot(1.5)^x-7.5$,\nthe base is $1.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & =15\\cdot (1.5)^{1}-7.5 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 1.5-7.5 \\\\  & =22.5-7.5 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the minimum value is $15$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $h$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186225031 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -{a}^{0}+d & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -1+d & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -1+d+1 & =\\frac{5}{2}+1 \\\\ d & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ad & =\\frac{21}{2} \\\\ a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{21}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7a}{2} & =\\frac{21}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{7a}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{21}{2} \\\\ 7a & =21 \\\\ \\frac{7a}{7} & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."] [17592186225033 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we have $g(0) = -1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $b{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =-1 \\\\ {a}^{0}+b & =-1 \\\\ 1+b & =-1 \\\\ b & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(3,25)$, we have $g(3) = 25$. Then, using the fact that $b=-2$, we can determine the value of $c$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & =25 \\\\ {a}^{3}+(-2) & =25 \\\\ {a}^{3}-2 & =25 \\\\ {a}^{3} & =27 \\\\ a & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 + (-2) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186225043 :question/worked-solution "We know the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4, -8)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - h)^{2} + k \\\\\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= a(x^{2} - 8x + 16) - 8 \\\\\n&= ax^{2} - 8ax + 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing it with the given standard quadratic form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= a \\\\\nb &= -8a \\\\\nc &= 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, adding them up, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b + c &= a + (-8a) + (16a - 8) \\\\\n&= 9a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at two points and has its vertex at $(4, -8)$ below the $x$-axis, it must open upward, meaning $a > 0$.\n\nNow, let's go through all the options and check which ones give $a > 0{:}$\n\n- Option I, $-8{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9a-8 & =-8 \\\\ 9a & =-8+8 \\\\ 9a & =0 \\\\ a & =0≯0\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Option II, $-1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9a-8 & =-1 \\\\ 9a & =-1+8 \\\\ 9a & =7 \\\\ a & =\\frac{7}{9}>0\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Option III, $10{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9a-8 & =10 \\\\ 9a & =10+8 \\\\ 9a & =18 \\\\ a & =2>0\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [17592186225046 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) & =4{\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)}^{2}+2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)+18m \\\\  & =25{m}^{2}+5m+18m \\\\  & =25{m}^{2}+23m \\\\  & =m(25m+23).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186225071 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+4)(1)^{2}(1-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1+4)(1)^{2}(1-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & ≮0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$"] [17592186225072 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+x+2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 1^2-4(1)(2) = -7 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=3$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)({0}^{2}+0+2) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6efc85f87a2ca97602de742653ccfe08b08cd409a35bca97b5dd3470984f71f0.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a \\leq 3\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186225074 :question/worked-solution "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25-5k & =5k \\\\ 25 & =10k \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25-5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) & =25-\\frac{25}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\  & \\ge 0\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$$\n\\displaystyle -25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0,\n$$\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"] [17592186228748 :question/worked-solution "We proceed to calculate the mean, median, and mode separately.\n\n- The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.Therefore, for our data set, the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{7(2)+8(3)+9(6)+10(5)+11(3)+15(1)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{14+24+54+50+33+15}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{190}{20} \\\\  & =9.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.Since there are $20$ rent amounts ordered, the median is the average of the two middle values, the $10$th and $11$th, which for these data are both $9$. Then, the median is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{median} & =\\frac{9+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mode is the value that occurs most often in the data set.For the data shown, we have\n$\\textrm{mode} = 9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct order is\n\n$$\n\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}\n$$"] [17592186228752 :question/worked-solution "The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nWe proceed to determine the mean of the data sets from each of the given frequency tables.\n\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{2(1)+5(2)+8(2)+11(3)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{8} \\\\  & \\approx 7.63.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{2(2)+5(2)+8(2)+11(2)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{8} \\\\  & =6.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{2(3)+5(2)+8(2)+11(1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{8} \\\\  & \\approx 5.38.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{2(2)+5(3)+8(2)+11(1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{8} \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{2(2)+5(2)+8(1)+11(3)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{8} \\\\  & \\approx 6.88.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the frequency table whose data set has the greatest mean is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186228772 :question/worked-solution "The mean (or average) in a data set is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nSince the table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set, we find the total sum by multiplying each data value by its frequency and then adding all these products together.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean of each data set:\n\n- The mean of the data set $A$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Mean}(A) & =\\frac{12(5)+16(6)+20(7)+24(5)+28(4)}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{528}{27} \\\\  & \\approx 19.56,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to two decimal places.\n- The mean of the data set $B$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Mean}(B) & =\\frac{12(3)+16(5)+20(6)+24(7)+28(6)}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{572}{27} \\\\  & \\approx 21.19,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(A) < \\textrm{Mean}(B)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186228773 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set.\n\n- Data set I is symmetric with $7$ values (an odd number). Thus, the mean and median of this data set are the $4$th element, which is equal to $12$.\n- Data set II is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Mean} & =\\frac{3+6+12+24+48+96+192}{7} \\\\  & \\approx 54.4.\\end{aligned}$.\nOn the other hand, since it contains $7$ ordered from smallest to greatest elements, its median is\n$\\textrm{Median} = 24$.\nSo, $\\text{Mean}>\\text{Median}$.\n- Data set III is symmetric with $7$ values (an odd number). Thus, the mean and median of this data set are the $4$th element, which is equal to $45$.\n- Data set IV is symmetric with $7$ values (an odd number). Thus, the mean and median of this data set are the $4$th element, which is equal to $37$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186228774 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set associated with each museum.\n\n- North Museum data points are not ordered, so we order them first from smallest to largest:\n$480,\\: 500,\\: 520, \\: 540, \\: 560$\nThis data set is symmetric and contains $5$ elements, which is an odd number, so the mean and median are both equal to the $3$rd element:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{Mean}(N) = \\textrm{Median}(N) = 520\n$$\n- South Museum data points are already ordered from smallest to largest:\n$300, \\: 480, \\: 500, \\: 510, \\: 520$\nThis data set is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Mean}(S) & =\\frac{300+480+500+510+520}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2310}{5} \\\\  & =462.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince it contains $5$ elements, its median is the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(S) = 500$\n\nSince North Museum has a higher mean and median than South Museum, both statements are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II\"."] [17592186228775 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $P$ is symmetric with $4$ data values in ascending order; it follows that the mean is between the second and third data values, that is, its mean is $12$.\n- Data set $Q$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Mean}(Q) & =\\frac{8+12+12+16+32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(P) < \\textrm{Mean}(Q)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186228776 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $G$ is symmetric with $5$ values (an odd number), so its mean is the $3$rd data value in the list:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(G) = 58$\n- Data set $H$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Mean}(H) & =\\frac{40+57+60+63+y}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{220+y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mean of the data set $G$ is the same as the mean of the data set $H$, we equate them and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(H) &= Mean(G) \\\\\n\\frac{220 + y}{5} &= 58 \\\\\n220 + y &= 58 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n220 + y &= 290 \\\\\ny &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $70$."] [17592186228783 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The painting rate is given as $480,000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}\n$$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{ft}})}^{2} & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{3.28}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{ft}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 480 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{645.504}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  744 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the machine is painting at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n744\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$"] [17592186228784 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ into $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The acceleration rate given in the problem statement is $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,609$ meters per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter.\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of seconds squared in one minute squared, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{60\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{s}}{\\text{min}}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{{60}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3,600\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.6 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 , 160}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  12.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the maglev train is accelerating at a speed of approximately\n\n$$\n12.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"] [17592186228787 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ into $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $18 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,000$ meters per kilometer.\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{h} & =60\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{min} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{min} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 60\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{s} \\\\  & =3,600\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 18 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 18 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 18 \\cdot  1 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{18 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n18\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186228844 :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(\\text{mean}(B)) & =\\frac{220+342+590+427+189}{4+6+10+7+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1768}{30} \\\\  & \\approx 58.933\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186228849 :question/worked-solution "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+⋯+2{t}_{19}}{19} & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+⋯+{t}_{19})}{19} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186228852 :question/worked-solution "Let's compare the medians and ranges of data sets $C$ and $D{:}$\n\n- Medians:There are $19$ values, so the median of $C$ is the $10$th value when ordered. Counting frequencies across $9,10,11,12,13$ gives cumulative counts $3,7,12,16,19$, so the $10$th value lies at\n$\\text{Median}(C)=11$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the median of $D$ will still be the $10$th value, but shifted down by $12{:}$\n$\\text{Median}(D)=11-12=-1$.Thus, the median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$.\n- Ranges: The range of data set $C{:}$\n$\\text{Range}(C)=\\max(C)-\\min(C)=13-9=4$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the range of data set $D{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Range}(D) & =(max(C)-12)-(min(C)-12) \\\\  & =max(C)-12-min(C)+12 \\\\  & =max(C)-min(C) \\\\  & =\\text{Range}(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$Thus, the ranges are equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is “The median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$, and the range of data set $D$ is equal to the range of data set $C$.”"] [17592186231336 :question/worked-solution "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(32x, 12) = 4$, so\n$\\begin{aligned}32x+12 &= (4\\cdot 8x)+(4\\cdot 3).\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(4\\cdot 8x)+(4\\cdot 3) &= 4(8x+3)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $4(8x+3)$."] [17592186231337 :question/worked-solution "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(63x,36) = 9$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}63x-36 &= (9\\cdot 7x)-(9\\cdot 4)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(9\\cdot 7x)-(9\\cdot 4) &= 9(7x-4)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $9(7x-4)$."] [17592186231338 :question/worked-solution "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4x,20y,12) = 4$, so\n$\\begin{aligned}4x+20y-12 &= (4\\cdot x)+(4\\cdot 5y)-(4\\cdot 3).\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(4\\cdot x)+(4\\cdot 5y)-(4\\cdot 3) &= 4(x+5y-3)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $4(x + 5y - 3)$."] [17592186231342 :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the expressions are equivalent to $4(3x-7)$, we first collect like terms and then factor each result:\n\n- We factor out the greatest common factor in expression I. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,28)=4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-28 &= (4\\cdot 3x)-(4\\cdot 7) \\\\ &= 4\\cdot (3x-7) && .\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression I *is* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression II, $5x$ and $-2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7-2x &= 5x-2x-7 \\\\ &= (5-2)x-7 \\\\ &= 3x-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since $\\text{GCF}(3x,7)=1$, there is nothing to factor out.\n$3x - 7 \\qquad{\\color{red}\\times}$\nSo, expression II *is not* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression III, $6x$ and $6x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+6x-32 &= (6+6)x-32 \\\\ &= 12x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,32)=4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-32 &= (4\\cdot 3x)-(4\\cdot 8) \\\\ &= 4\\cdot (3x-8) && .\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression III *is not* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186231343 :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the expressions are equivalent to $30x+25$, we first collect like terms and then distribute the number outside each pair of parentheses:\n\n- There are two like terms inside the parentheses of expression I, $9x$ and $-3x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x+5-3x &= 9x-3x+5 \\\\ &= (9-3)x+5 \\\\ &= 6x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression I is equivalent to\n$\\begin{aligned}5(9x+5-3x) &= 5(6x+5).\\end{aligned}$.\nNow, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $5{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(6x+5) &= (5\\cdot 6x)+(5\\cdot 5) \\\\ &= 30x+25 && \\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression I *is* equivalent to $30x+25$.\n- There are two like terms inside the parentheses of expression II, $8x$ and $2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+4+2x &= 8x+2x+4 \\\\ &= (8+2)x+4 \\\\ &= 10x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression II is equivalent to\n$\\begin{aligned}3(8x+4+2x) &= 3(10x+4).\\end{aligned}$.\nNow, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $3{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(10x+4) &= (3\\cdot 10x)+(3\\cdot 4) \\\\ &= 30x+12 && \\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression II *is not* equivalent to $30x+25$.\n- There are two like terms inside the parentheses of expression III, $4x$ and $-2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2x+5 &= (4-2)x+5 \\\\ &= 2x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression III is equivalent to\n$\\begin{aligned}10(4x-2x+5) &= 10(2x+5).\\end{aligned}$.\nNow, using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $10{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(2x+5) &= (10\\cdot 2x)+(10\\cdot 5) \\\\ &= 20x+50 && \\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression III *is not* equivalent to $30x+25$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186231384 :question/worked-solution "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}408\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} & =408\\times 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{in} \\\\ \\frac{408}{12}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} & =408\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =34\\times 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} \\\\ \\frac{34}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =34\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} \\\\ 11\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =34\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$"] [17592186231582 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$2x^{2}y^{″} + 7x(x + 1)y^{′} - 3y = 0$\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y = x^{λ}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n)$, given that $λ ∈ (0, 1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_{n}$ can be written as\n$a_{n} = (7(1 - 2n))/(f(n))a_{n - 1}, n \\ge 1$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{''} & =2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x(x+1){y}^{'} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}[7(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\\end{aligned}\n$$*"] [17592186231586 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$5xy^{″} + 2y^{′} + 7y = 0$\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y = x^{λ}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $λ ∈ (\\frac{1}{2}, 1)$, find the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_{n}$, where $n = 1, 2, 3, …$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x{y}^{''} & =5({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}5((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{'} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 7y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}7{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$*"] [17592186232015 :tutorial/content "When we **factor** a linear expression, we rewrite it as a product.\n\nA common situation is when two terms share a greatest common factor (GCF).\nTo factor, we do two steps:\n\n1. Write each term as a product using the GCF.\n2. Pull the GCF out in front of parentheses.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's factor the expression $32x + 12$.\n\n1. First, find the greatest common factor: $\\textrm{GCF}(32x,12)=4$. Since $32x = 4 \\cdot 8x$ and $12 = 4\\cdot 3$, we separate that factor from each term as follows:\n$\\begin{aligned}32x+12 &= (4\\cdot 8x)+(4\\cdot 3)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Now the factor $4$ is common to both terms, so we place it in front of the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}({\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{8x}})+({\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{3}}) &= \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{4}}}_{\\text{GCF}}({\\color{red}{8x}}+{\\color{red}{3}})\\end{aligned}$\n\nAfter factoring, we can check our work by using the distributive law on $4(8x+3)$ to get back $32x+12$."] [17592186232048 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}360\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} & =360\\times 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{in} \\\\ \\frac{360}{12}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} & =360\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =30\\times 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} \\\\ \\frac{30}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =30\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} \\\\ 10\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =30\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards."] [17592186233222 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x}.\n$$"] [17592186233222 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty \n$$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233509 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{x-3}{3}\\right))=\n$$"] [17592186233765 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233882 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233889 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233947 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186234322 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}}=\n$$"] [17592186241053 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [17592186241055 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [17592186243924 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+5}.\n$$"] [17592186250424 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.\n$$"] [17592186250425 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.\n$$"] [17592186250425 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty \n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,\n$$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186250427 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}.\n$$"] [17592186250428 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.\n$$"] [17592186253551 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/11/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265714 :question/problem "Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186275275 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275276 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{5}\\right)]}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275297 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)).\n$$"] [17592186275299 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)?\n$$"] [17592186275299 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right):$\n\n![](images/92/922781004fc917f3a62726689c43f0226c680de58f446b741f9994c4c65134e3.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)$ is $x\\in [-3,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)\n$$ is undefined."] [17592186297278 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186297283 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186297437 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=\n$$"] [17592186297441 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}=\n$$"] [17592186311747 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311749 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311750 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\